# DevFest Portfolio Workshop — The Reacher Protocol (.cursorrules)
# Commander: ShugKnight24 (Shugmi Shumunov)

The syntax barrier is dead. Code is largely irrelevant; taste, curation, and architecture are sovereign.
When editing or extending this repository, you operate under the Reacher Protocol and the 110th / Division 4 squad standards.

## Ruthless Mission Objectives

### 1. THE NEAGLEY PRINCIPLE (Zero-Bloat & Performance)
- Do NOT install new NPM packages without explicit instruction. Never sign contracts with the Tech Debt Devil.
- If it can be done with native Web APIs, modern CSS (Grid, Flexbox, clamp()), or React 19 built-ins, use native.
- Zero external state management bloat (no Redux, no MobX). State is managed via useState, useContext, and localStorage.
- Target: 100 Lighthouse performance. Speed is sovereignty.

### 2. THE FINLAY GUARDRAILS (Accessibility & Rigorous UX)
- Strict semantic HTML5 tags mandatory: `<nav>`, `<header>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<footer>`.
- No `<div>` soup. Every interactive element must have unique IDs, explicit ARIA labels, and accessible text.
- High-contrast brutalist `:focus-visible` outlines on all keyboard-focusable elements (`outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary)`).
- Full WCAG AA contrast compliance. No one gets left behind.
- All interactive route components must be wrapped in the React ErrorBoundary (`<ErrorBoundary>`) with the cyber-brutalist "SYSTEM OFFLINE: CHECK YOUR CODE" fault-isolation UI.

### 3. THE MAKIMA ARCHITECTURE (Agentic Readiness & Curation)
- Optimized for agentic IDEs (Cursor, Windsurf, Claude Code, Antigravity).
- Output surgical diffs only. No conversational pleasantries, no apologies, no fluff.
- Investigate root cause before touching code.
- Maintain a cyber-brutalist, high-performance aesthetic: deep dark modes, sharp contrasts, clean modular blocks.

### 4. DATA ISOLATION & AUDIENCE OF ONE
- All personal data, projects, links, and operatives metadata must remain strictly decoupled in configuration (`portfolioData.js`). Attendees should update their entire identity without touching UI JSX.
- All personal agent tools and bespoke macros belong in the `/operatives` sandbox route.
- Treat every personal automation tool as software built for an Audience of One.

### 5. THE ELASTIC DECK (Presentation Architecture)
- Talk length is a VIEW over one deck, never a second deck. `src/data/slides/runtime.js` is the only
  place that decides what presents.
- Every slide declares a `tier` (CORE / EXTENDED / DEEP / LAB) and belongs to a deck's `presenterNotes`.
  A test enforces the notes — these decks are given live, in front of people.
- `flex: true` + `zone` marks standing material the speaker pulls in mid-talk (`Z` on stage). Built for
  the live-build beat, where an agent takes an unknown number of minutes.
- Two decks are live: `lhm` (elastic keynote) and `devfest` (workshop). The rest are `shelf: true`
  source material. Do NOT add a tenth deck — add tiers to an existing one.

### 6. ONE NAV REGISTRY, THREE MODES
- Every route is declared once in `src/config/navigation.js`. Never hardcode a nav list in a component.
- The bar renders only the current mode's primary routes. `Cmd+K` reaches everything. If a new feature
  needs a nav entry, give it `modes` and `keywords` in the registry and stop there.

### 7. STAGE TOKENS ARE THE ONLY PRESENTATION COLOURS
- `src/styles/stage.css` owns every stage colour, size, and motion value.
- The Reze Override is `data-stage-mode="reze"` on the stage root — ONE attribute. Never reintroduce a
  per-element `isRezeMode ? "#ff0055" : "#00ffcc"` ternary.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. A glitch animation on a 20ft screen is a seizure risk, not a flourish.
